#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148590} :activity/id #uuid "57371a77-6667-5bdd-b857-9b52cc9a92ac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048313 :topic/id #uuid "93cbb547-bfa6-5649-8003-160f883bcb4f" :topic/math-academy-id 2467} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147455 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147392 :question/id #uuid "4447e034-350f-5e6b-a501-0f57a3888bd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8975"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4196423-3401-54e1-b214-9dbc4861cfe1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414710}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Spheres, Hemispheres, Cones, and Cylinders"} :step/id #uuid "2317aeba-3862-5ebe-a106-96c1e216914d" :step/math-academy-id 33549 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147611 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147290 :question/id #uuid "319d252c-a8c4-5a5f-ad2b-f4bdb5702b75" :question/math-academy-id "e-2662"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8fd3c2c-3340-50df-a95c-3b82f165a071" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050585}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147899}] :knowledge-point/title "Naming Prisms"} :step/id #uuid "19df8c7e-89cb-5578-919d-65b12d903d26" :step/math-academy-id 8908 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148595}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147612 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147291 :question/id #uuid "0583c82a-049c-51e6-aebc-bcd32423824e" :question/math-academy-id "e-2668"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9d83422-a877-5de8-bc55-2c7aae9eeb72" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414712}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Polyhedra"} :step/id #uuid "a01f473c-01ac-5a25-a94d-294c2cc662a2" :step/math-academy-id 8914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148714 :tutorial/content "A **polyhedron** is a $3$-dimensional shape where each side is a polygon. These flat polygonal sides are called **faces**.\n\n**Prisms** are polyhedrons made of two identical polygonal faces (called **bases**) connected by equal parallel line segments. If these line segments are perpendicular to the bases, we have a **right** prism.\n\nLet's take a look at some common prisms.\n\n- A **triangular (right) prism** consists of two identical triangular faces connected by equal parallel line segments.\n\n![](images/95/9563bd4f9c40ff5921998ed5840f8e5b43fb1af579a64a06a14782be4a86ece2.png)\n\n- A **rectangular (right) prism** consists of two identical rectangular faces connected by equal parallel line segments. Notice that each face is a rectangle.\n\n![](images/39/39ea3dd56722c7fb05ba275a054ff46479e3ee96851dafdd66fe09455a517f1a.png)\n\n- A **cube** is a special case of a rectangular prism that consists of six equal square faces.\n\n![](images/41/415d03a9a886793eee4b4d16e50af26002c0881279481b2e70773b8b7bff02a5.png)\n\nBy adding more sides to the polygonal base, we can make more prisms. For example, the following shape is a pentagonal prism.\n\n![](images/8f/8f4dd8fe8c120eabbe5a1ad6413f6f0cd9cd3d22efe8b13e25ac3d2a916f3258.png)\n\nWe can also construct hexagonal prisms, heptagonal prisms, etc." :tutorial/id #uuid "af52fff0-b45a-59d7-85f8-cacc3a9cb9f9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2202 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a54ee23c-aa2f-55da-8cdb-fd96e504ff67" :step/math-academy-id 8915 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148718 :tutorial/content "**Pyramids** are polyhedrons made of a polygonal base, with the other triangular faces meeting at a single point.\n\nLet's take a look at some common pyramids.\n\n- A **triangular pyramid** has a triangular base with $3$ other triangular faces that meet at a single point.\n\n![](images/d8/d8597d1e526e2af72255f7dce1cc13e01cf76f1c53522003b126be67a0a6c55d.png)\n\n- A **rectangular pyramid** has a rectangular base with $4$ triangular faces that meet at a single point. If the base is a square, we call this a **square pyramid.**\n\n![](images/26/26700acaf9eae64227988ddcfb43269c0eba6f6447229ec8854cc1ebfeab3334.png)\n\n- A **pentagonal pyramid** has a pentagonal base with $5$ triangular faces that meet at a single point.\n\n![](images/4c/4c73b984b3e4a2ccb0796646ebb4c547947fab7359914599d928a63819818725.png)\n\nSimilarly to prisms, we can add more sides to the polygonal base to make more types of pyramids." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ebd5b00-1afa-5ee5-9321-47a3e76e71ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2248 :tutorial/title "Pyramids"} :step/id #uuid "0e005ac2-7765-5ed0-83c6-dc038809de33" :step/math-academy-id 9198 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148588}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147615 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147294 :question/id #uuid "dc558592-e65f-5369-890d-fffc633f2a3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-2757"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddd0beef-ff0c-5ef3-88c4-ce49fbb8d5cb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495110}] :knowledge-point/title "Naming Pyramids"} :step/id #uuid "4c62acce-9aa5-51e7-aab3-ddef11ded75b" :step/math-academy-id 9199 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148719 :tutorial/content "Not all three-dimensional shapes are polyhedrons. Let's see some examples.\n\n- A **sphere** is the set of points with the same distance to a fixed point called the **center.** A sphere is a $3$-dimensional analog of a circle. A **hemisphere** is half of a sphere.\n\n![](images/84/84e7b63b5ec8761d4a3ac8a00b15a08f0dab7d4f95a536721df0c82e2999873f.png)\n\n- A **cylinder** is a solid shape with two parallel circular bases. There are two types of cylinders.A cylinder is **right** if the axis that passes through the centers of the circular bases is perpendicular to the bases. Otherwise, the cylinder is **oblique.**\n\n![](images/f8/f8ddb161d2d8628dd1294cc0d0042437300787217b518be73f010e79be90dcf7.png)\n\n- A **cone** is a solid shape with one circular base and one vertex called the **apex**. There are two types of cones.A cone is **right** if the axis that passes through the center of the circular base and the vertex is perpendicular to the base. Otherwise, the cone is **oblique.**\n\n![](images/2f/2f9e9ca18a36336ed99695bb51b167ffbd83f002139728f8dfe15227bf9f7745.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "daf27141-b818-5e08-b09c-b940a0ab763a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2250 :tutorial/title "Other Three-Dimensional Shapes"} :step/id #uuid "9570e433-348e-5b8c-8ac5-b5e75693bf4e" :step/math-academy-id 9201 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148323}}] :activity/title "Identifying Three-Dimensional Shapes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147667}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414718} :activity/id #uuid "4fbea89c-8f67-5118-a1de-ee1d485d0bea" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048313 :topic/id #uuid "93cbb547-bfa6-5649-8003-160f883bcb4f" :topic/math-academy-id 2467} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b8744a0-0044-51b5-ba74-cb20ab4ca8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Heptagonal pyramid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414682} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd46a53-d530-5265-b868-97591886f8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Triangular pyramid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414683} {:answer/id #uuid "259cae62-6586-502d-bdf1-ccc26c4cad6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Cube" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414684} {:answer/id #uuid "2687c571-e8a9-5532-9d9f-a9a82d818067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Heptagonal prism" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414685} {:answer/id #uuid "5737e950-c7ba-532c-b742-a233972b8975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Hexagonal prism" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414692}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414682} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4290576-9979-5457-9533-5ecbf8f69d51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a6fd9de3-145f-5992-abae-ba4ddaa8590f" :question/math-academy-id "q-93195" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b5055d5290bfefa599d5fa357b76a3ccff05f9b0330c6610ce77deba949e34f.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the polyhedron shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The solid is a **heptagonal pyramid** since it is a pyramid whose base is a heptagon.\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not a triangular pyramid since not all its faces are triangular.\n\n- The solid is not a heptagonal prism since it has only one heptagonal face.\n\n- The solid is not a hexagonal prism because it has no hexagonal face.\n\n- The solid is not a cube since it has triangular faces."} :step/id #uuid "0b209725-9e0d-563b-a187-f60cb609d0c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414717}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a030b9-4c33-563f-932d-cdf0103434a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6a/6a0e4fa080407695749f4e204f08cc062828f358573d56b2bc7e8a25fb61c2c6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414680} {:answer/id #uuid "40e3dace-c8f0-5451-8326-07ac6adb342d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/18/180c9fc2f5c00706683921067bd3d4ced0e9f514df89ac1de66d1c959efd81cc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414689} {:answer/id #uuid "488d6f82-d92d-5984-aa98-caa3998bb08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9b/9b094303688730cbd49075ad75faa3705e00999d40f253addfda88365991e401.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414691} {:answer/id #uuid "b17cb05e-a4c9-58a6-946a-d88e0d16f2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/50/50679b5a5b333daae4346bf729ac2791a1024baed9ae580918d37925c11fe7f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414697} {:answer/id #uuid "be92cffe-3554-5972-9564-de1dcf9aba22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9f/9feb81a7255725b4d1b370012db8b7f06981daea8c96f16aa5fc6a59ae0a8898.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414698}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414691} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e137d3c-1309-5a42-8f77-b44643406daf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "60a685f7-2a5b-5185-bd48-1abde4b2de57" :question/math-academy-id "q-69873" :question/problem "Which of the following is **not** a polyhedron?" :question/worked-solution "Each face in a polyhedron must be a polygon. Therefore, the only option that is **not** a polyhedron is:\n\n![](images/9b/9b094303688730cbd49075ad75faa3705e00999d40f253addfda88365991e401.png)\n\n    \n\nNotice that its left face is not a polygon (it's a circle)."} :step/id #uuid "4d46733c-4460-50dc-859b-7dd20a320ad8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414716 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eb7f412-2103-58ff-a13b-06c1c4330e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/92/92151e8fc81b3c8b689b62ed0e5cf810b54f953d5a5675261b44d25d8419402a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414688} {:answer/id #uuid "44ffa52e-4d0a-566e-8284-0b15eba04159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d7/d79beefbd1605daf59fdb152c541784bd886019cc759d52dd6a1e92ffa3a0ff3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414690} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbbb49d-5c4b-58fb-ace1-178d1cd464f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/95/9563bd4f9c40ff5921998ed5840f8e5b43fb1af579a64a06a14782be4a86ece2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414695} {:answer/id #uuid "c68b03d2-d134-50f4-b325-4d5ddd31ff0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/03/037f72666076a57b9d20d9ec39a6c2e6efab31156a81d00ed419c6799c7056b4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414700} {:answer/id #uuid "e351cfcb-2aac-5397-b2e7-de491bc69f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0b/0bd12e1073334c21ebd437e103c55d166b4d433d0eec46787bb1906014c0c41d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414702}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414702} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03ee3f1-4549-55c9-919e-e76bc4e6846f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c22d74f-c382-5fcc-9b04-2bd8cc4168b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-117697" :question/problem "Which of the following is **not** a polyhedron?" :question/worked-solution "Each face in a polyhedron must be a polygon. Therefore, the only option that is **not** a polyhedron is:\n\n![](images/0b/0bd12e1073334c21ebd437e103c55d166b4d433d0eec46787bb1906014c0c41d.png)\n\n              \n\nNotice that some of its faces are not polygons (they have curved sides)."} :step/id #uuid "7bebf769-82ed-5ee5-a479-5d4cd518d762"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a28560e-80a3-57af-9dfa-e817b929bb85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414687} {:answer/id #uuid "7cda9107-2b9b-5255-8de4-8a7f274925d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Right cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414696} {:answer/id #uuid "c366b12c-e909-516f-a328-bcc2b653608e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Oblique cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414699} {:answer/id #uuid "ccfdca69-53cd-540a-b90a-39a52dccc997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Oblique Cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414701} {:answer/id #uuid "ed96ecff-7047-526b-aea2-13644f032520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Right cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414704}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414704} :answer-field/id #uuid "8963e5d2-5f7d-506c-a501-21c462d94bc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2449ad0f-383b-597c-b298-3099a99567bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-107276" :question/problem "![](images/26/264fb9324cad3bbfba25aa5a72c9c4a44e6c149b82f69d9e101fdff9e57a05d2.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the solid shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The solid is a **right cone**. \n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The solid has one circular base and one vertex. So, it's a cone.\n\n- The axis that passes through the center of the circle and the vertex is perpendicular to the base. So, it's right."} :step/id #uuid "87f1f045-6509-52aa-9286-2d5c94a46705" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414716}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414718 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1900d6b5-ab4e-5cb6-875c-d1325629dba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Cube" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414681} {:answer/id #uuid "332e4dbe-20b8-54c1-9358-14a060035cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Pentagonal prism" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414686} {:answer/id #uuid "58cc569c-846f-5023-b24c-d641fdf00ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Triangular pyramid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414693} {:answer/id #uuid "598ea44a-33fd-558b-a72c-9415a8e71931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Pentagonal pyramid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414694} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a1f23e-e7bc-5e52-a9a0-4fe70a04cb29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Rectangular prism" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414703}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414694} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5df61c8-1685-5b05-bfab-2db504a8dcb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0def774f-f257-5952-add7-f3a6aeb741ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-61656" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c73b984b3e4a2ccb0796646ebb4c547947fab7359914599d928a63819818725.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the polyhedron shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The solid is a **pentagonal pyramid** since it is a pyramid whose base is a pentagon.\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not a triangular pyramid since not all its faces are triangular.\n\n- The solid is not a pentagonal prism since it has only one pentagonal face.\n\n- The solid is not a rectangular prism since not all of its faces are rectangles. Due to the same reasons, the solid is not a cube."} :step/id #uuid "d58ea3ef-a954-5458-b665-9faf042fb48e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414715}}] :activity/title "Identifying Three-Dimensional Shapes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414679}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116148} :activity/id #uuid "abceb987-e559-5c80-8a21-441171253b50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048419 :topic/id #uuid "9d572d01-c598-509f-894d-a4f2b6bee64e" :topic/math-academy-id 1584} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116148 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116503 :tutorial/content "In general, for $k>0$,\n\n- the graph of $y=f(x-k)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=f(x)$ by $k$ units to the *right*, while\n- the graph of $y=f(x+k)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=f(x)$ by $k$ units to the *left*.\n\n**Watch out!** It may seem counterintuitive that subtracting $k$ moves the graph to the right, while adding $k$ moves the graph to the left. However, this is in fact true.\n\nTo see why, let's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. Let's use this graph to plot the graph of $y=f(x-2)$.\n\n![](images/6c/6ca96bba855899f9b87ae06c166503c9af739fcd6bb2ec466a73b75cc70dde7f.png)\n\nLet's take some points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ and find the corresponding points on the graphs of $y=f(x-2)$.\n\nFor example, take the point $(3,-4)$. The fact that it's on the graph of $y=f(x)$ means that\n\n$$\nf(3)=-4\n$$\n\nThen if\n\n$$\nx={\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nf(x-2) = f({\\color{red}5}-2) = f(3) = -4\n$$\n\nwhich means that $({\\color{red}5},-4)$ is on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$.\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$ with the same $y$-value and a $x$-value that is $2$ units *greater*! Several more examples of points are shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math] & Points on [math]y=f(x-2)[/math] \\\\ (3,-4) & (5,-4) \\\\ (1, 2) & (3, 2) \\\\ (0, 1.5) & (2, 1.5) \\\\ (-2, 0) & (0, 0)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's plot both curves.\n\n![](images/ed/edae088984432dc7d0fff816a9e016ad221a3c897a78cf79efe6c784fa9176fe.png)\n\nWe see that every point on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$ is shifted $2$ units *to the right* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "da231b95-be14-58c3-a8fe-6261d61c7d83" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3280 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6345469f-1236-5eaf-8f5f-c51d47bf4f3c" :step/math-academy-id 18017 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116149}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115259 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115088 :question/id #uuid "e320a97a-d524-5225-895e-76034e599fa9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4973"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "372e7248-7fe0-5138-896d-61b4866f83cd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050647}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115507}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Horizontal Translation of a Given Graph"} :step/id #uuid "d171b522-79a1-5dbc-bcf1-aa7179ecd080" :step/math-academy-id 18018 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116150}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116150 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115260 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115089 :question/id #uuid "4dec57af-1c02-56fe-b38e-e62795fdc93d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4974"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "919da198-25e0-5b6f-8d99-79f1508759b9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115622}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Corresponding Point on a Horizontally Translated Function"} :step/id #uuid "b5c16920-e146-5ef0-841d-e2645a4e1de6" :step/math-academy-id 18019 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116151}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116504 :tutorial/content "When graphing a function, we can combine vertical and horizontal translations. In general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(x-h)+k\n$$\n\nis a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $h$ units to the right and $k$ units up.\n\nIt doesn't matter which translation we do first, but it's often easiest to start with the horizontal translation." :tutorial/id #uuid "ceb4fcff-ae84-5217-aa0c-16d64fc23eac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3282 :tutorial/title "Combining Vertical and Horizontal Translations"} :step/id #uuid "c6cc0af1-c408-599c-ac0a-69b7af985fac" :step/math-academy-id 18020 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115261 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115090 :question/id #uuid "86b96055-a0e1-5dc7-9f89-364f77412a77" :question/math-academy-id "e-4975"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7c84dd5-2c76-5584-81a1-53d2459afb94" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233127}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting Horizontal and Vertical Translations of a Given Graph"} :step/id #uuid "b18fd01e-e160-5b17-a9cf-730b2b261357" :step/math-academy-id 18022 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116174}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116174 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115103 :question/id #uuid "740d5deb-3982-5293-9071-ddaaa189fb81" :question/math-academy-id "e-5229"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57ef1e2c-07d4-5d04-9de8-8884cae17c00" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115621}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Corresponding Point on a Horizontally and Vertically Translated Function"} :step/id #uuid "5cdd1f56-3cc3-5455-896a-aec5448b7269" :step/math-academy-id 19261}] :activity/title "Horizontal Translations of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115418}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158376} :activity/id #uuid "986c9303-5c90-5d19-85f8-915c37c8bfb0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048423 :topic/id #uuid "9ddcc703-a984-5097-a142-236dc3c2fbaf" :topic/math-academy-id 2979} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158376 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158530 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nx \\cdot 3^x - 2 \\cdot 3^x = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {3}^{x}-2\\cdot {3}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)\\cdot {3}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx-2  = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad 3^x=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x-2=0$ has the solution $x=2.$\n\n- The equation $3^x=0$ has no real solution because $3^x$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has a single solution, $x=2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "90c9dc44-008d-5e8d-b233-b33802547a9c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4715 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "56edca54-b7a1-571d-b42c-21eaa3456fa1" :step/math-academy-id 467336 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158377}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158377 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157437 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157170 :question/id #uuid "d6fce272-8b91-53f1-bfa4-32f098d8ee86" :question/math-academy-id "e-7836"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394779}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "f532c29e-5c06-5b5f-8c3b-8a42ad9ae43e" :step/math-academy-id 467337 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158378}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158378 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157171 :question/id #uuid "f1a4b9b1-a6fd-504b-882f-df32959c35ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-7838"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386578}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "942b92f8-26b9-51c3-94de-c738c9f145af" :step/math-academy-id 467340}] :activity/title "Solving Exponential Equations Using the Zero-Product Property" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157527}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185019} :activity/id #uuid "f4c9f3cd-60b1-585d-8adf-32b7b25fe2df" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048796 :topic/id #uuid "bfab9d57-0ef9-5cb2-ad40-9f6fefe847aa" :topic/math-academy-id 3845} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184193 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184091 :question/id #uuid "7d8d292e-a062-5370-bb53-87f637fef4f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7621"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0aa53b10-4f0e-59b6-b00a-f5bd07a34b14" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048186}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184865}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Several Transformations: Horizontal Shift Only Inside Parentheses"} :step/id #uuid "24f23941-c700-549c-9117-5700b1664f45" :step/math-academy-id 28410 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185031}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185348 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ to which we apply the following transformations in the order listed:\n\n1. translation by $\\color{blue}5$ units up\n2. translation by $\\color{red}4$ units left\n3. stretch by a scale factor of $\\color{purple}3$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\nWhat is the final function expression after applying all the transformations?\n\nTo find the final expression, we apply each transformation in turn.\n\n1. Translating the graph of $y=f(x)$ up by $\\color{blue}5$ units is equivalent to adding $\\color{blue}5$ to the function expression. Applying this transformation, we obtain $y=f(x)+{\\color{blue}5}$.\n2. Translating the graph of $y=f(x)+5$ by $\\color{red}4$ units to the left is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $(x+{\\color{red}{4}})$. Applying this transformation, we obtain $y =f(x+{\\color{red}4})+{\\color{blue}5}$.\n3. Stretching the graph of $y=f(x+4)+5$ by a scale factor of $\\color{purple}3$ parallel to the $y$-axis is equivalent to multiplying the function expression by $3$. Applying this transformation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 3 \\cdot (f(x + 4) + 5) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot f(x + 4) + 3 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 3f(x + 4) + 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final function expression is\n\n$$\ny=3f(x+4)+15\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6e9cbac2-104e-55b2-a0ca-d481f24c1cc6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6206 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f7f231fe-9e17-5908-8d88-384170536e99" :step/math-academy-id 37620 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185020 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184227 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184133 :question/id #uuid "afb9f795-d566-52e9-9b92-2046918e5d73" :question/math-academy-id "e-9988"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c76594da-4831-56c4-8c49-68349ba62a19" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233460}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Several Transformations: Horizontal Stretch Followed By Horizontal Shift Inside Parentheses"} :step/id #uuid "16c93c37-5e32-5491-a3b7-a262662bf8fe" :step/math-academy-id 37621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185031 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184234 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183916 :question/id #uuid "2ea5d035-862f-514f-94bb-9b703d97a04f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10016"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7797323b-5c5c-55db-b2d9-4184556dc18a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184863}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Several Transformations: Horizontal Stretch Only Inside Parentheses"} :step/id #uuid "457bac5a-22a8-5ad9-9412-de0bf90d6a87" :step/math-academy-id 37731 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185032}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184235 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183917 :question/id #uuid "5cd41918-0c78-503d-a741-e6e7f15a3a96" :question/math-academy-id "e-10017"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2252e35-f1c6-5d83-853a-9090c253fd35" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184864}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Several Transformations: Horizontal Shift Followed By Horizontal Stretch Inside Parentheses"} :step/id #uuid "69065bb9-317b-55a5-84c9-5770500328b2" :step/math-academy-id 37732 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185020}}] :activity/title "Constructing Functions Using Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184429}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088782} :activity/id #uuid "f0473c05-670a-51ba-9160-5bd79f73bcef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048841 :topic/id #uuid "c542186c-8ef4-51c0-ba33-93814e2f0b6d" :topic/math-academy-id 847} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088782 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088935 :tutorial/content "The **remainder** theorem states that for any polynomial $p(x)$ and number $c$, when we divide $p(x)$ by $(x-c)$, the remainder is equal to $p(c)$.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^3-5x^2-7x+3\n$$\n\nand the number\n\n$$\nc={\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's divide $p(x)$ by $(x-{\\color{red}{2}})$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $4$ | $-5$ | $-7$ | $3$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $8$ | $6$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $4$ | $3$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n\nWe get a remainder of ${\\color{blue}1}$. Indeed, when we work out $p({\\color{red}2})$, we also get a result of ${\\color{blue}1}$, just like the remainder theorem guarantees:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(2) &= 4(2)^{3} - 5(2)^{2} - 7(2) + 3 \\\\\n&= 32 - 20 - 14 + 3 \\\\\n&= 1✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo understand why this happens, notice that we can use the result of the synthetic division to rewrite $p(x)$ as\n\n$$\np(x) = (4x^2+3x-1)(x-2)+{\\color{blue}{1}}\n$$\n\nWhen we substitute\n\n$$\nx={\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we see that the $(x-2)$ factor turns to $0$, thereby eliminating the quotient $(4x^2+3x-1)$ and leaving only the remainder of ${\\color{blue}{1}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(2) &= (4 \\cdot 2^{2} + 3 \\cdot 2 - 1)(2 - 2) + 1 \\\\\n&= (4 \\cdot 2^{2} + 3 \\cdot 2 - 1)(0) + 1 \\\\\n&= 0 + 1 \\\\\n&= 1✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe remainder theorem remains valid even if we substitute $(x-c)$ with $k(x-c)$, as long as $k$ is a non-zero real number. Let's look at an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "ec2e08af-eeae-5a50-a66d-3a58ff9e13f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3725 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7c1bfca3-cf4e-5357-af6e-be27f040c32b" :step/math-academy-id 472891 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088783}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087854 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087581 :question/id #uuid "1e79a137-05cd-5dd5-bb0a-04c9040c05e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5881"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "18823139-c8c2-56ad-b8cc-4e43b97e904d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051061}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088327}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of the Remainder When a Polynomial is Divided by a Linear Factor"} :step/id #uuid "06f9b02e-9420-5f1e-96a8-6d8bf989b0c6" :step/math-academy-id 472892 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088784}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088784 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087855 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087582 :question/id #uuid "488cb495-bf1e-5f52-87a5-8bfc6fbbce16" :question/math-academy-id "e-5882"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0dab780-3669-5234-8c5b-4b7f844f3a8f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088335}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Single Unknown in a Polynomial Given a Remainder"} :step/id #uuid "8a4aebf0-0079-5718-8019-64198fe0dbd6" :step/math-academy-id 472896 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088785}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088785 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087856 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087583 :question/id #uuid "6bbc5788-0449-51cb-815a-83f578ef6b15" :question/math-academy-id "e-5883"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed971935-4ef9-5c49-a67d-26dde1a350bc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049060}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512889}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Values of Two Unknowns in a Polynomial Given Two Remainders"} :step/id #uuid "4f0d6002-9b2f-516f-84c2-e0269816f303" :step/math-academy-id 472900}] :activity/title "The Remainder Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087918}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193719} :activity/id #uuid "b6520ec4-4c2b-5d03-b5d7-fdb5074438f4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048898 :topic/id #uuid "ca45e315-1972-57c0-8e15-999d27a51d68" :topic/math-academy-id 3944} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193719 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193826 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to compute inverses of reciprocal functions.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the function $f(x)$, given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{7}{2x+4}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{7}{2x+4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{7}{2y+4}\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{7}{2y + 4} \\\\\nx(2y + 4) &= 7 \\\\\n2xy + 4x &= 7 \\\\\n2xy &= 7 - 4x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{7 - 4x}{2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{7 - 4x}{2x}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1be3fc12-b770-5a76-ab56-1385a320d85f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6512 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c89b0360-91da-5424-aedb-da76a815447b" :step/math-academy-id 466026 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193720}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192405 :question/id #uuid "0042ce26-8793-5690-b4ab-9f018afe979a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10459"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "421b9396-2a7b-5c45-897a-9de5f52683de" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050908}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275199}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Reciprocal Function"} :step/id #uuid "0da282af-995a-5faf-b0d0-876baec229e5" :step/math-academy-id 466027 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193721}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193721 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192406 :question/id #uuid "9b149c51-015f-5346-9fca-8da406d0a79a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10460"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7266d5c7-67b9-59db-b994-07dcb143af1f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051079}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390552}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Simple Rational Function"} :step/id #uuid "cde1c192-24a5-5dc6-a1eb-22b02b4431e9" :step/math-academy-id 466031 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193722}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193722 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192816 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192407 :question/id #uuid "f6ab6606-77ce-583a-9712-175825864612" :question/math-academy-id "e-10461"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcedd9d0-f87c-58b2-9ef1-c9fa0ef2e5c1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294877}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Unknown Value Given the Inverse of a Rational Function"} :step/id #uuid "44d32822-3dee-5d1f-bc85-937a6b36e438" :step/math-academy-id 466034}] :activity/title "Inverses of Reciprocal Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192864}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199235} :activity/id #uuid "5e22fbe6-6f4f-523d-81b5-4e8eb335e94e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048982 :topic/id #uuid "d1016931-94b0-5e15-aaf8-b42aa5ff778d" :topic/math-academy-id 4236} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199235 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199598 :tutorial/content "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{5x + 2y =-1; - 4x - 3y =-2\n$$\n\nSuppose we want to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system using the elimination method.\n\nFirst, notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the *first* equation is ${\\color{red}{5}}$, while in the *second* equation it is $-{\\color{blue}{4}}$.\n\nSo, if we multiply the *first* equation by ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ and the *second* equation by ${\\color{red}{5}}$, then the $x$-coefficient in the first and second equations respectively will become $20$ and $-20$, and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{(5x + 2y =-1) \\cdot (4); (-4x - 3y =-2) \\cdot (5)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20x + 8y &= -4 \\\\\n- 20x - 15y &= -10 \\\\\n(8y - 15y) &= (-4 - 10) \\\\\n- 7y &= -14 \\\\\ny &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have successfully eliminated the variable $x$ from the system." :tutorial/id #uuid "e0fe50e3-a9ab-5917-9101-61d27d052fc8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8705 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3efd94e5-0a3a-5721-9e17-4cc42a121334" :step/math-academy-id 196706 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199236}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198350 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198216 :question/id #uuid "b618dc4c-996c-501c-972c-1bcfdeee32be" :question/math-academy-id "e-13998"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "772da46a-8b9c-54e7-8661-a5865b1df4e3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198910}] :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the First Variable"} :step/id #uuid "88185242-c507-5397-b525-337a9cbf0f57" :step/math-academy-id 196707 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199237}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198351 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198217 :question/id #uuid "68da240e-2c79-50d7-86dd-d203d01dcb32" :question/math-academy-id "e-14001"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cf591e2-45d5-580c-9d46-c6a8645c0513" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198912}] :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the Second Variable"} :step/id #uuid "d2ed5ea1-20b9-594f-9470-adebce7bf262" :step/math-academy-id 196710 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199238}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199238 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198352 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198218 :question/id #uuid "c04ac5ff-446a-52d8-b464-88d38efd6088" :question/math-academy-id "e-14003"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829fbee-ca0a-5cf7-b232-c2c6869cb2a6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512908}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the First Variable"} :step/id #uuid "067db66a-f3df-588b-a8d5-4ed1d9ae12b4" :step/math-academy-id 196713 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199239}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199239 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198353 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198219 :question/id #uuid "10d6a803-5114-5995-87c3-1b0e0e87a079" :question/math-academy-id "e-14004"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fc5a393-5fcd-59c9-993b-d900b790e141" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198997}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"} :step/id #uuid "8e9862dd-b298-5880-b133-f1e0adff0ef5" :step/math-academy-id 196716}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: Two Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198509}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148335} :activity/id #uuid "982ccfc9-c613-5d9e-8291-be7273b41b36" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049083 :topic/id #uuid "d9335c65-9755-52f0-8ecc-68ac966800f0" :topic/math-academy-id 2534} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148692 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a *positive* geometric sequence where the second term is $4$ and the fourth term is $64$. How do we find the common ratio $r$?\n\nThis is a little trickier than before because we're not given two consecutive terms, but let's try to figure it out. First, let's denote\n\n$$\na_2 = 4, \\qquad a_4 = 64\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by:\n\n$$\na_n = a_1r^{n-1}\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=2$ and $n=4$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2} &= a_{1}r^{2 - 1} = a_{1}r \\\\\na_{4} &= a_{1}r^{4 - 1} = a_{1}r^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing that $a_2 = 4$ and $a_4 = 64$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1}r &= 4 \\\\\na_{1}r^{3} &= 64\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have a system of equations to solve. To solve this system, we *divide* the *second* equation above by the *first* equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a_{1}r^{3}}{a_{1}r} &= \\frac{64}{4} \\\\\n\\frac{a_{1}r^{3}}{a_{1}r} &= \\frac{64}{4} \\\\\n\\frac{r^{3}}{r} &= 16 \\\\\nr^{2} &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve the above equation for $r$. Note that $16 = 4^2$, and so we can solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= 16 \\\\\nr^{2} &= 4^{2} \\\\\nr &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since our sequence must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the common ratio $r=4$." :tutorial/id #uuid "37612557-7324-573c-b33f-5503374cbd61" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2013 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c5a853ac-bca8-588c-9552-d62691b2b1cb" :step/math-academy-id 456842 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148336}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148336 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147461 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147230 :question/id #uuid "84364858-da8e-5241-85ca-ec84057bc6e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-2179"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81a78a4d-f4ef-5c51-b34e-22d3ee51c7c1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050067}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148113}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation for the Common Ratio"} :step/id #uuid "a9dbb1d0-1aa6-5b1b-8d37-cd6baf4d3f42" :step/math-academy-id 456843 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148337}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148337 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148693 :tutorial/content "Finding the common ratio by solving a system of equations is quite a lot of work. Is there an easier way?\n\nIndeed there is. Given two terms of a geometric sequence $a_n$ and $a_m$, the formula for $r$ is as follows:\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate how this works. Suppose that we have a geometric sequence whose second term is $3$ and whose fifth term is $192$. What is the common ratio?\n\nHere, we're given $a_5 = 192$ and $a_2 = 3$. Substituting $n=5$ and $m=2$ into the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{5 - 2} &= \\frac{a_{5}}{a_{2}} \\\\\nr^{3} &= \\frac{192}{3} \\\\\nr^{3} &= 64\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve the above equation for $r$ by noting that $64 = 4^3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{3} &= 64 \\\\\nr^{3} &= 4^{3} \\\\\nr &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the common ratio is $r=4$.\n\nWhen dealing with an equation that is **cubic** in $r$ (i.e., contains an $r^3$), then there is only one possible answer, unlike the squared case ($r^2$) where we have to pick from either positive or negative." :tutorial/id #uuid "e368a435-c6f1-5ff3-b7dc-76122c25a574" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2015 :tutorial/title "Finding the Common Ratio Using a Formula"} :step/id #uuid "8d4fd6a5-b7a1-5431-ac86-c98ca494cd68" :step/math-academy-id 456846 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148338}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148338 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147462 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147226 :question/id #uuid "9d8383ad-c617-5b01-8055-214a8c639f12" :question/math-academy-id "e-2175"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "631bc93f-4596-526f-9652-f35137b60883" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050828}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148111}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Ratio Using a Formula Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms"} :step/id #uuid "376f6145-6352-593b-bfb2-743f3f279fe9" :step/math-academy-id 456847 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148339}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148339 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147463 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147229 :question/id #uuid "7aa4b1ba-4362-5a4b-88d4-25d9ebe2cef7" :question/math-academy-id "e-2178"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df9158c-01a9-5596-9528-6033296b2925" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234163}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Ratio Given Some Additional Information About the Geometric Sequence"} :step/id #uuid "29dd91b7-618d-590b-82d4-ba8d0996e7fe" :step/math-academy-id 456850}] :activity/title "Finding the Common Ratio of a Geometric Sequence Given Two Terms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147713}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120590} :activity/id #uuid "9b3a1069-52b2-5215-b943-d2a9a9c9e0ec" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049225 :topic/id #uuid "e4d5c007-bb0e-5384-b435-d19add6ff444" :topic/math-academy-id 1745} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121005 :tutorial/content "The area of a circle with radius $r$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\pi r^2\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the area of a circle that has a radius of $3\\textrm{cm}$.\n\n![](images/ea/ea7b4221ec972df5bce524c031ac82ecd7f427aaf6fd04ce2a93734d9597d271.png)\n\nUsing the area formula, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n&= π \\cdot (3)^{2} \\\\\n&= π \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 9πcm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "81aba826-d72c-50d9-85e5-cb77544b3df7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2424 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2d8d663f-5c91-557f-836d-bff3e1edd11c" :step/math-academy-id 10882 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120591}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119571 :question/id #uuid "57ebd9c4-2338-5beb-8b58-0902de3d7f33" :question/math-academy-id "e-3183"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1aaab9fd-0ef0-5240-9142-d84b66df0eeb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050204}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235503}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Circle Given the Radius"} :step/id #uuid "ebfc68d5-4727-5d49-aafa-c697c47d0110" :step/math-academy-id 10883 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119572 :question/id #uuid "57533c91-7a96-52c7-b9a4-fee398a961b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3184"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcef46b2-41a9-58df-b11d-ef58f0b065a9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120482}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Circle Given the Diameter"} :step/id #uuid "86f2d07d-852b-50a6-a78c-64632ef6192f" :step/math-academy-id 10884 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120760}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119757 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119573 :question/id #uuid "41291efa-9dcd-5988-ac32-3a85f82bf5e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3186"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ad198fa-fde8-5d2a-baa5-a890951fb0df" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120485}] :knowledge-point/title "Areas of Circles: Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "930eef61-6f72-50c9-aa7a-49a4a5916ac3" :step/math-academy-id 10886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119861 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119676 :question/id #uuid "78a454c8-c08b-5c4a-8ff6-cd093296c6d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6804"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1740fa9-50b1-5fad-9f99-31820dd77067" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048187}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233302}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Radius or Diameter of a Circle Given the Area"} :step/id #uuid "f5e37c48-c83d-50eb-8302-fa4de1388ce0" :step/math-academy-id 25172 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120593}}] :activity/title "Areas of Circles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119984}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101796} :activity/id #uuid "af8776aa-b3ed-5305-95b5-bf42623f4ed3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049226 :topic/id #uuid "e4f7b310-649d-511b-ae6e-709dcea1f6cd" :topic/math-academy-id 1246} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100823 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100658 :question/id #uuid "0f441b48-b977-538c-82af-aa40ed4076d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-3561"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23567a86-8e1e-5b94-bac6-09c79cdb42c9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101388}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "2408ea61-ca9b-52c5-ae47-17af57a34e64" :step/math-academy-id 12001 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101797}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101603 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100824 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100659 :question/id #uuid "7a24a8e0-768e-541c-aff1-cad6f977da87" :question/math-academy-id "e-3562"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5feb914d-efad-5d89-85a9-db99befc9928" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050653}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235427}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule to Compute a Limit"} :step/id #uuid "a3d559f2-c362-56ee-804c-080f88071508" :step/math-academy-id 12002 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101602}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101796 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101928 :tutorial/content "The **product rule** states that the limit of the product of two functions equals the product of the limits.\n\nMore precisely, if\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, a}g(x)=K\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → a)(f(x) \\cdot g(x)) &= lim_(x → a)f(x) \\cdot lim_(x → a)g(x) \\\\\n&= L \\cdot K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we can evaluate\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,2} \\left[ (x^2 +2x +3)(x^3-2x-1) \\right]\n$$\n\nby evaluating the limit of each polynomial separately, and then multiplying the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)[(x^{2} + 2x + 3)(x^{3} - 2x - 1)] &= lim_(x → 2)(x^{2} + 2x + 3) \\cdot lim_(x → 2)(x^{3} - 2x - 1) \\\\\n&= (2^{2} + 2 \\cdot 2 + 3) \\cdot (2^{3} - 2 \\cdot 2 - 1) \\\\\n&= (4 + 4 + 3) \\cdot (8 - 4 - 1) \\\\\n&= (11) \\cdot (3) \\\\\n&= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c7f68019-2904-5740-87e6-e66d189cb771" :tutorial/math-academy-id 175 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "55b6421a-40da-531f-9d53-41329827a526" :step/math-academy-id 333 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101603}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101797 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101929 :tutorial/content "The **quotient rule** states that the limit of the quotient of two functions equals the quotient of the limits (provided that the limit of the denominator is not zero).\n\nMore precisely, if\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}g(x)= K \\neq 0\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \\dfrac{\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)}{\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}g(x)}=\\dfrac{L}{K}\n$$\n\nFor example, we can evaluate\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,2} \\dfrac{x^2+4x}{x+1}\n$$\n\nby evaluating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, and then dividing the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)\\frac{x^{2} + 4x}{x + 1} &= (lim_(x → 2)(x^{2} + 4x))/(lim_(x → 2)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2^{2} + 4 \\cdot 2}{2 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{3} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "46d526ad-25bf-58f5-88e0-695b4ff6d430" :tutorial/math-academy-id 178 :tutorial/title "The Quotient Rule for Limits"} :step/id #uuid "67532783-b8e9-5de2-9ca2-19a49fbf6ec4" :step/math-academy-id 339 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101798}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101798 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100952 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100620 :question/id #uuid "04538f07-6e4c-547e-be0c-8b62025e19ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-28"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a649e49-73c7-5470-8361-2002da74f234" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235468}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule to Compute a Limit"} :step/id #uuid "e8f2b1af-f0df-5d06-b349-280fb90373de" :step/math-academy-id 340 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101799}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101799 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100953 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100621 :question/id #uuid "71b3f83c-33e6-5043-9215-7db7b4b12497" :question/math-academy-id "e-29"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0159149-010c-5bef-a28d-85c4c43f0586" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512873}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "1da33225-c9cd-5fd9-8576-b2d8fdce7dcf" :step/math-academy-id 342}] :activity/title "The Product and Quotient Rules for Limits" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101087}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097737} :activity/id #uuid "886871c8-0b6e-56ce-bc7f-54affec4fd88" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049360 :topic/id #uuid "eed250d1-ce5b-5073-a193-140fd0562d65" :topic/math-academy-id 1104} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097833 :tutorial/content "A **unit vector** is a vector whose magnitude is equal to $1$.\n\nGiven any vector $\\mathbf a$, we can create a unit vector $\\mathbf u$ by dividing $\\mathbf a$ by its magnitude $\\mid \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, \\mid$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\n![](images/c7/c78d5b75c1d5928e7b269a4a67229487b3d3324e9815b11c5fd1f47f63437d82.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "a90fd4b3-d41f-589f-9067-39d57e3e8712" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2906 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "961ff0d0-9cef-5a2d-8207-63481c0cc1ab" :step/math-academy-id 430121 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097738}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096447 :question/id #uuid "2a61b115-4e26-5966-b2a9-d2fedef6b728" :question/math-academy-id "e-4282"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a66a99-6b4a-5ad5-8922-6f48006ff183" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049715}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502322} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502319}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"} :step/id #uuid "e301e3f9-501f-5f63-afac-f53379b62c13" :step/math-academy-id 430122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096493 :question/id #uuid "6dfe09f0-30e2-53bc-a8f6-d7e4a3b8232a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5181"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b11642dd-80cc-539c-8c2d-8cdee58e9d3e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050315}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502429} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502428}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector in a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "957d3735-2041-5bec-9e7c-4c1623dd4899" :step/math-academy-id 430125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097740}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097740 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096448 :question/id #uuid "23d9a601-974b-51c2-a6ea-0ebadde19efc" :question/math-academy-id "e-4283"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44ac67b-5e3d-5b1e-bf2c-554b592ba29e" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502535} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502529}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Vector Sum"} :step/id #uuid "11ad8934-d3f9-546c-b161-9058d251ac66" :step/math-academy-id 430128}] :activity/title "Unit Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096884}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172879} :activity/id #uuid "9e60f656-c2ee-5768-b1af-a3eb8499887f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049387 :topic/id #uuid "f0b7f9f5-b45c-5478-b9c9-cc76a52e64dd" :topic/math-academy-id 3530} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173151 :tutorial/content "When finding the roots of a transformed radical function, we need to consider whether the radical's index is odd or even.\n\nLet's start with the odd case first. As an example, we will find the root of the function $f(x)$, given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sqrt[{\\color{blue}{3}}]{10-4x} + 2\\,\n$$\n\nNotice that we're dealing with a cube root, and so the index of the radical is ${\\color{blue}{3}}$, which is odd.\n\nTo find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x} + 2 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x} + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x} &= -2 \\\\\n(\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x})^{3} &= (-2)^{3} \\\\\n10 - 4x &= -8 \\\\\n- 4x &= -18 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{9}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac92\n$$\n\nA transformed radical function with an odd index will always have one real root." :tutorial/id #uuid "a49268e2-e58c-5966-ba98-b1786827ba76" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4864 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ce36d68a-bbb4-56c0-b92d-852e3f103282" :step/math-academy-id 30460 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172023 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171829 :question/id #uuid "64957847-edce-52b1-9848-545c18014262" :question/math-academy-id "e-8143"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e6159dd-c12e-5882-be17-815b4cc9ae52" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294900}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function With an Odd Index"} :step/id #uuid "9a817a13-cf12-5a56-abd1-7fbe139fe7b4" :step/math-academy-id 30461 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172928}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172024 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171830 :question/id #uuid "96c06afd-4449-594e-8655-63f43ede736c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8144"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d059c56-ae7f-5fb6-8bc6-2ce97bb7f876" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404544}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function With an Even Index"} :step/id #uuid "9b6bd47e-dc8a-5923-bbcc-1f0390d2d1db" :step/math-academy-id 30463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173170 :tutorial/content "Let's now learn how to find the root of a radical function with an even index. For example, let's try to find the root of\n\n$$\nf(x) = -3\\sqrt[4]{10x+1} + 9\n$$\n\nTo find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt[4]{10x+1} + 9 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1} &= -9 \\\\\n\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1} &= 3 \\\\\n(\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1})^{4} &= 3^{4} \\\\\n10x + 1 &= 81 \\\\\n10x &= 80 \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3\\sqrt[4]{10 \\cdot 8 + 1} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3\\sqrt[4]{81} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3 \\cdot 3 + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n0 &= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=8$.\n\nWhen the index of the radical function is even, we should always check for extraneous roots by substituting them back into the original equation.\n\nSometimes, a radical function with an even index has no root. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "0a1feb60-62da-5279-8bfd-f8be6c389de7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5161 :tutorial/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function: Even Case"} :step/id #uuid "213cee54-dfad-5696-a210-97d588e61069" :step/math-academy-id 32450 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172881}}] :activity/title "Roots of Transformed Radical Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172204}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120588} :activity/id #uuid "a1420d3a-15f6-5a48-8abd-1c63acae3d69" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049407 :topic/id #uuid "f1e03cee-ef99-5d00-a73c-6d65d6b68b14" :topic/math-academy-id 1848} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119751 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119567 :question/id #uuid "927b97a7-437e-5c46-b83d-1e8d2d62007e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3155"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24d0f296-256c-5da4-8992-e2112dfe99b6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050517}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120493}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing Angles in the Coordinate Plane With the Unit Circle"} :step/id #uuid "3ccb485c-5163-55c9-925d-e843ae0e9b1e" :step/math-academy-id 10716 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120585}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119752 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119568 :question/id #uuid "02cd545d-7fde-520b-924e-5ff4f8e4244b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3156"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6435eb94-76f8-5e46-b005-03451ab6eba2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050053}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120491}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing Angles in Radians in the Coordinate Plane With the Unit Circle"} :step/id #uuid "233228e9-30e5-5f8d-9ef2-3ab6bbe1665a" :step/math-academy-id 10717 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120587}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119753 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119569 :question/id #uuid "50e22612-2f22-5c48-a2da-a6a0580375a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-3157"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50de296d-56a1-5166-aa7d-42de027a33bc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050847}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512902}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Trigonometry to Find an Angle in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "ccdf0b34-4b6d-556e-854b-ad4fe1251a2b" :step/math-academy-id 10718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120587 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121003 :tutorial/content "The unit circle turns out to be a really useful tool to help us to understand the trigonometric functions. However, sometimes, we'd like to represent an angle in the coordinate plane without reference to the unit circle.\n\nThankfully, the standard way that we represent angles in the coordinate plane without reference to the unit circle is pretty much the same as with the unit circle!\n\nFor example, the angles $45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, 225^\\circ$, and $315^\\circ$ can be represented in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n![](images/31/317690d0489b8a5f5cd9e360c9d5894a539908b00ffc50ae0b70bfe03708162b.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "365c6b7d-1c9d-5476-a76c-1ae37c55c79d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2410 :tutorial/title "Representing Angles in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "89777c8f-a2e8-5a31-80c5-4f9068dc913b" :step/math-academy-id 10720 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121004 :tutorial/content "The **unit circle** is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/13/138322240b05231ce56ca195a06724da8a1c05ec4701ac1d9d49cadc9129c8c0.png)\n\nThe unit circle is an essential tool for understanding trigonometry.\n\nSuppose that we want to construct an angle $\\theta$ in the coordinate plane. There is a standard way in which we do this.\n\n- First, we draw the unit circle.\n- Then, we represent $\\theta$ as a central angle of the unit circle. The angle has its vertex at the origin, its initial side lies on the positive $x$-axis, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nFor example, we represent the angles\n\n$$\n\\theta = 45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, 225^\\circ\n$$\n\nand $315^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1d4419633933ba2b5874873f625ca9016d42c2dc9a685c9db3619daf8512fde5.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e10ff02b-0d3a-58a5-813d-2072db6f706f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2411 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7a8a6a1b-9549-51bb-b895-d289b410b7c1" :step/math-academy-id 10728 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119754 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119570 :question/id #uuid "98f445f6-806d-5169-81f9-183b93e5bb94" :question/math-academy-id "e-3161"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04ac62a3-1302-5011-937b-9fb20cb7fe95" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120337}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing Angles in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "05ddd3b3-6529-5e19-bcb5-8d016c5bee75" :step/math-academy-id 10750 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120586}}] :activity/title "Angles in the Coordinate Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120042}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088513} :activity/id #uuid "6a1ef665-fde3-5f3b-8a1c-833507155bc9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049589 :topic/id #uuid "02b0bd28-fe3c-5e27-b64f-dcec4176a6ea" :topic/math-academy-id 818} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087683 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087491 :question/id #uuid "effc520d-4717-5e46-9358-c2ea34c7aa24" :question/math-academy-id "e-3990"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d82c39c-5ec6-5cf3-96f8-e778091a451a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088119}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions Containing Natural Logarithms Using a Calculator"} :step/id #uuid "f04afc6a-ec7b-5bb9-8934-3d5425695f14" :step/math-academy-id 14035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087684 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087492 :question/id #uuid "97f9f0d6-d948-5f9b-b0ae-57e6a456a53b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4056"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30497207-86c5-507f-906a-d23bd0af71e9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049865}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088260}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Exponential and Logarithmic Form With Natural Logarithms"} :step/id #uuid "31c5a04f-e9fd-55b5-89a0-ebedce9a81b6" :step/math-academy-id 14408 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088503}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087685 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087493 :question/id #uuid "fd02e00f-9e2c-54f1-a5d3-fb0da9c9a413" :question/math-academy-id "e-4057"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a323ad55-d029-58f4-b0a3-cc10b7dab125" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048964}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088262}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Natural Logarithms"} :step/id #uuid "d60c198f-7395-53e7-9b80-37df76e919d2" :step/math-academy-id 14409 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088512}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088512 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087692 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087497 :question/id #uuid "37ca3b87-9f5f-583c-95ff-83ca35608559" :question/math-academy-id "e-4155"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "169ca81a-c698-553c-9f47-d6c4303b82a9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512917}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Natural Logarithms of Fractions and Roots"} :step/id #uuid "2d52a820-958f-501f-b903-bbb1725d6a6c" :step/math-academy-id 15037 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088501}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088513 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088888 :tutorial/content "**Euler’s number**, denoted $e$, is one of the most important constants in mathematics. It's an irrational number, and its numerical value is approximately\n\n$$\ne\\approx2.718\\,281\\,828\n$$\n\nThe **natural logarithm**, denoted $\\ln$, is a special logarithm whose base is equal to Euler's number:\n\n$$\n\\ln a = \\log_e a\n$$\n\nIn other words, the natural logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $\\ln{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = e^x$\n\nEverything we've done with logarithms can be applied to the natural logarithm.\n\nFor instance, we can express\n\n$$\ne^3 \\approx 20.086\n$$\n\nin logarithmic form. If we compare\n\n$$\ne^{3} \\approx 20.086\n$$\n\nwith $a = e^x$, we get $a = 20.086$ and $x = 3$. Therefore,\n\n$$\ne^3\\approx 20.086\\quad \\textrm{is equivalent to}\\quad \\ln(20.086) \\approx 3\n$$\n\nNote:\n\n- Scientific calculators usually have a special key that allows $\\ln a$ to be computed for specific values of $a$.\n- Euler's number shows up in many different areas, including compound interest, population growth, and calculus. For now, it’s enough to know that $e$ is a natural base for logarithms and exponentials. We’ll meet this number often in future lessons, where its deeper significance will become clear." :tutorial/id #uuid "b07e75a3-c350-5268-ad01-a8b5fb789147" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2868 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "89fbb86b-6ce2-55ba-a735-e867abf24645" :step/math-academy-id 15308 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088502}}] :activity/title "The Natural Logarithm" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087902}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125322} :activity/id #uuid "7d6120fd-aa08-5562-9120-ce173e8fe311" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049731 :topic/id #uuid "0f127f4a-3cf5-5736-90b8-9817b73eb050" :topic/math-academy-id 1889} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125322 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125752 :tutorial/content "A function is **invertible** on an interval if and only if it's one-to-one. In other words, it must pass the horizontal line test.\n\nNot all functions have inverses! For example, consider the function $f(x)=x^2-1$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/e9/e99832fa1867a8314dda20f392d0c897ebb2942ac91a652ab1cf474b5d2d9612.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not a one-to-one since it does not satisfy the horizontal line test.\n\nTo find the graph of a function's inverse, we usually reflect it over the line $y=x.$ However, if we reflect $f(x)$ over the line $y=x,$ the result is not a function since it will not satisfy the vertical line test.\n\n![](images/7a/7a5a95f1cc3e2c8b40e865a24d22f4529e47c3c0137a6cb8d0369059f4a89a56.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is *not* invertible.\n\nIf a function is not invertible, we can sometimes make it invertible by restricting its domain.\n\nHere, if we take $f(x)=x^2-1$ over the restricted interval $x \\in (0,\\infty),$ then it passes the horizontal line test and we can find the corresponding inverse function by reflecting the graph of $y=f(x)$ over the line $y=x.$\n\n![](images/0a/0a7f98c2dce4599ee74cc13dc53c3a693b9c3716ffe54c708811f4c011f18b6d.png)\n\nTo determine if a function is invertible over a particular domain, we need to use the horizontal line test to check if it's a one-to-one function." :tutorial/id #uuid "90b8b9a7-2b60-5a13-9829-c02575ad8c16" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2768 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5b61f81e-5366-54d7-b4dc-eb12246fa126" :step/math-academy-id 13908 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125323}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124472 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124283 :question/id #uuid "454d081a-fdcb-5101-9f85-0e3b27986695" :question/math-academy-id "e-3961"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050525}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403251}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "619ae142-fadd-53d9-975f-a485d1f363b6" :step/math-academy-id 13909 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125324}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125324 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124284 :question/id #uuid "b6722faa-6069-59c1-8861-75b8207b0a90" :question/math-academy-id "e-3962"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323865}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "067f6188-2d0b-5a28-8513-b138cb5eda82" :step/math-academy-id 13911 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125325}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125325 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124285 :question/id #uuid "7c28a136-0b86-5d13-a5f8-b0dd0e0ea1ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-3963"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049716}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396687}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"} :step/id #uuid "956e822f-5a25-5d3b-ac84-ada9c2c8dee8" :step/math-academy-id 13916}] :activity/title "Invertible Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124694}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093230} :activity/id #uuid "e6f89105-7e4b-5912-9b01-20e1a6810bb5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049755 :topic/id #uuid "1125135d-4341-5e5d-9065-381cc0460203" :topic/math-academy-id 942} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093195 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093471 :tutorial/content "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\nP(x) = 2x^{\\color{blue}4} - 3x + 1, \\qquad\nQ(x) = -6x^{\\color{red}3} + 5x^2 + x\n$$\n\nNotice that these polynomials have degrees $4$ and $3,$ respectively. We write this as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P) = {\\color{blue}4}, \\qquad\n\\textrm{deg}(Q) = {\\color{red}3}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, \n\n- the leading term in $P(x)$ is $2x^{\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the leading term in $Q(x)$ is $-6x^{\\color{red}3}$\n\nAs a result, the leading term in the product $P \\cdot Q$ equals the product of the leading terms of $P$ and $Q\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n2x^{\\color{blue}4} \\cdot (-6x^{\\color{red}3}) = -12x^{{\\color{blue}4}+{\\color{red}3}} = -12x^{\\color{purple}7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be ${\\color{purple}{7}}.$\n\nWe have the following general rule:\n\n*The degree of the product of two polynomials equals the sum of the degrees of these polynomials:* \n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P \\cdot Q) = \\textrm{deg}(P) + \\textrm{deg}(Q)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60187490-7cd6-566d-ab45-128e2c4f81c3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6201 :tutorial/title "The Degree of a Product of Two Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "f1dac5f5-12ea-56be-8067-1b9f815159f5" :step/math-academy-id 37591 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093196}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093196 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092312 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092159 :question/id #uuid "d0f6fda4-4083-5541-8f0e-9a61f740ba8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9981"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049729}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435059}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "7e843dd4-2f8a-5237-87b2-28b5a09adb3d" :step/math-academy-id 37594 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093197}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093197 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093472 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the degree of the sum and difference of two polynomials $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nFirst, let's suppose that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ have *different* degrees. In this case, the degree of the sum or difference equals the largest of the two degrees. We write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P \\pm Q) = \\max \\big\\{ \\textrm{deg}(P), \\textrm{deg}(Q) \\big\\},\n$$\n\n \nwhere the $\\textrm{max}$ function simply returns the largest element of the set.\n\nFor example, suppose that $\\textrm{deg}(P) = 5$ and $\\textrm{deg}(Q) = 2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P + Q) = \\max \\big\\{ 5, 2 \\big\\} = 5.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo see why this is true, note that the leading term of $P$ contains an $x^5,$ and the leading term of $Q$ has an $x^2.$ When we add the two polynomials, no term of $Q$ could cancel out the leading term of $P.$ So the sum of the polynomials must have degree $5.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fae91d47-b786-5c2f-9a29-dfb06624930f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6202 :tutorial/title "Degrees of Sums and Differences of Polynomials: Case 1"} :step/id #uuid "82947aff-7f67-51bc-9a88-6e5d204e1c08" :step/math-academy-id 37596 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093199}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093198 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093473 :tutorial/content "Polynomials are also closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication but not closed under division.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following two polynomials:\n\n$$\nP(x) = x+1, \\qquad Q(x) = x^2\n$$\n\n- When we *add* the two polynomials, we get a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P+Q & =(x+1)+({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =x+1+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+x+1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- When we *subtract* the two polynomials, we get a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P-Q & =(x+1)-({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =x+1-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+x+1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- When we *multiply* the two polynomials, we get a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P\\cdot Q & =(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot {x}^{2}+1\\cdot {x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- However, *dividing* the two polynomials does *not* give a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{P}{Q} & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** Although polynomials are not closed under division, the ratio of two polynomials is *sometimes* a polynomial. For example, if $P=x^2$ and $Q=x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{P}{Q} =  \\dfrac{x^2}{x} = x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial." :tutorial/id #uuid "d529e51a-742c-52f5-874b-546cf98a1849" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6228 :tutorial/title "Closure Properties of Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "2236e99f-a53e-57c2-91ab-c3091e8a86a9" :step/math-academy-id 37829 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093231}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093199 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093474 :tutorial/content "Now suppose that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ have the *same* degree. That is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P) =   \\textrm{deg}(Q) = n.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn this case, the degree of the sum or difference can be *any integer* from $0$ to $n.$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\textrm{deg}(P \\pm Q) \\leq n\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- Let $P$ and $Q$ be polynomials of degree $15.$ Then, the *largest possible degree* of $P+Q$ is $15$ since the resulting polynomial can't contain terms of order higher than $15.$\n\n- On the other hand, the *smallest possible degree* of $P+Q$ is $0.$ For instance, suppose $P=x^{15}$ and $Q=1-x^{15}.$ Then $P +Q = 1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f9d9fe12-e873-5b5b-aca8-f212f9be62b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6229 :tutorial/title "Degrees of Sums and Differences of Polynomials: Case 2"} :step/id #uuid "6b4c7081-0037-5d22-8c22-bf18957c01d2" :step/math-academy-id 37830 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093232}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093230 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093380 :tutorial/content "When we add, subtract, or multiply two integers, the answer is always an integer. \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following two integers:\n\n$$\na = 3, \\qquad b=2\n$$\n\n- When we *add* the two integers, we get an integer:\n\n$$\na + b = 3+2 = 5\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- When we *subtract* the two integers, we get an integer:\n\n$$\na - b = 3-2 = 1\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- When we *multiply* the two integers, we get an integer:\n\n$$\na \\cdot b = 3\\cdot 2 = 6\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- However, dividing the two integers does *not* give an integer:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac32 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol\\times}}\n$$\n\nWe say the integers are **closed** under addition, subtraction, and multiplication but **not closed** under division.\n\n**Watch Out!** Although integers are not closed under division, the ratio of two integers is *sometimes* an integer. For example, if $a=4$ and $b=2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} =  \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2,\n$$\n\nwhich is an integer." :tutorial/id #uuid "a7d0a253-b58a-5205-b05d-d5d2f1d49f6e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1593 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "90c92812-0369-53f2-8139-3a602c9d8ddf" :step/math-academy-id 4197 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093198}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093231 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091946 :question/id #uuid "5ae8b304-5ce3-5520-8893-92a02784155d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1316"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050827}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435053}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "11713b46-54cf-58b5-8dc5-2984d413ed5f" :step/math-academy-id 4198 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093195}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093232 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092328 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091947 :question/id #uuid "53557831-390f-5b8b-b5c3-6634d425c04d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1317"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435058}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "bd94fe81-ec77-53b8-8ef8-e461ac344d67" :step/math-academy-id 4201 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093233}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093233 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091948 :question/id #uuid "621d4d9b-ebfb-56ef-9614-807077aea4c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-1318"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435055}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "cabcbccd-aabb-5b9e-a421-ce222693f535" :step/math-academy-id 4204}] :activity/title "Closure Properties of Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092437}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130150} :activity/id #uuid "15f10ca2-71ff-5122-8f23-034d870b20ee" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049796 :topic/id #uuid "1478960d-9b90-58d8-9424-023723660815" :topic/math-academy-id 2034} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130150 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130432 :tutorial/content "When adding numbers, it's usually possible to say whether the sum is rational or irrational.\n\nWe have three possible cases:\n\n- The sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational. Rational numbers can be represented as fractions of the form $\\dfrac ab$ (where $a$ and $b$ are integers), and adding two of these fractions always produces another fraction.\n- The sum of a rational and an irrational number is *always* irrational. To demonstrate, let's consider the following example:\n$\\sqrt 2 + \\dfrac12$\nIt's easy to see that this is irrational. Writing down the sum in decimal form, we get\n$1.414213562… + 0.5 = 1.914213562…$\nwhich is irrational since the numbers after the decimal point extend forever with no regular pattern.\n- The sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational! For example, the following sum is irrational:\n$\\sqrt 2 + \\sqrt 3 = 3.146\\,264\\,37\\ldots$\nHowever, the following sum is rational:\n$\\sqrt 2 + (-\\sqrt 2) = 0$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1b2b00f9-12fd-5755-9340-1a9477f61cdc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5941 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "37af3d14-d0da-5833-8cc2-8d34a27c4558" :step/math-academy-id 36260 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130161}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129235 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129054 :question/id #uuid "d6839658-bf90-55e5-845c-c3ded22c7735" :question/math-academy-id "e-9672"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf0b9c3-84ba-51c8-bb7e-a45a441834ea" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050300}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129452}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Sum or Difference of Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "8ae24fd7-3348-5c2c-97a8-bf4a352b05a5" :step/math-academy-id 36261 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130153}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129236 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129055 :question/id #uuid "bed9f966-a72e-56d4-acb9-3e156d763c96" :question/math-academy-id "e-9674"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b04804-a1f3-57de-b966-65074210fc6b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233424}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Product or Quotient of Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "6e1aa31d-da89-5630-8461-cb2becfe2266" :step/math-academy-id 36263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130433 :tutorial/content "When multiplying real numbers, it's usually possible to say whether the product is rational or irrational.\n\nWe have three possible cases:\n\n- The product of two rational numbers is *always* rational since the result must be a fraction of the form $\\dfrac ab$, where $a$ and $b$ are integers.\n- The product of a *nonzero* rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational. To demonstrate, let's consider the following example:\n$2\\cdot \\sqrt 2$\nIt's easy to see that this is irrational. Writing down the product in decimal form, we get\n$2 \\cdot 1.414213562… = 2.828427125…$\nwhich is irrational since the numbers after the decimal point extend forever with no regular pattern.\n- The product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational! For example, the following product is irrational:\n$\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{3}|= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 3}; = \\sqrt{6}$\nHowever, the following product is rational:\n$\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}|= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 2}; = \\sqrt{2^{2}}; = 2$" :tutorial/id #uuid "74425e69-1e7a-5c1c-b1ac-2aec2e2ad1df" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5942 :tutorial/title "Products of Rational and Irrational Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "f1b0c4be-3561-5047-b474-fc789baf401e" :step/math-academy-id 36266 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130162}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130161 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130437 :tutorial/content "To summarize:\n\n- the sum (or difference) of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- the sum (or difference) of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{irrational} = \\textrm{irrational}$\n- the sum (or difference) of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational." :tutorial/id #uuid "df9f71cf-81c8-546c-99f3-cca45192f745" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5998 :tutorial/title "A Summary"} :step/id #uuid "120df569-6a65-5f8d-9c7d-5fcfadd9b271" :step/math-academy-id 36625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130151}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130162 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130438 :tutorial/content "To summarize:\n\n- the product (or quotient) of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\times \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- the product (or quotient) of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\times \\textrm{irrational} = \\textrm{irrational}$\n- the product (or quotient) of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nFinally, it's worth pointing out that if $a$ is irrational, then the second rule implies that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1a\n$$\n\nis also irrational." :tutorial/id #uuid "45f6a1bf-6d91-5130-8aa5-a2d5863e2654" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5999 :tutorial/title "A Summary"} :step/id #uuid "d13633da-c1a6-540e-a940-eff75190b892" :step/math-academy-id 36626 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130152}}] :activity/title "Sums and Products of Rational and Irrational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129373}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120806} :activity/id #uuid "eed5aa9e-ed1f-5edb-990e-f8643f3d4226" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049878 :topic/id #uuid "1a524c38-2f30-5eb7-8ed4-12cf2dc92b46" :topic/math-academy-id 1753} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120806 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121085 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the rectangular prism below. How can we calculate its volume?\n\n![](images/94/94bdf8333e1cb6a7ff9f99bc4ef854433bc99a8310f61181309cf51709e7569c.png)\n\nIn general, we can find the volume $V$ of a rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV = lwh\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nl = 20\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad w=10\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad h=5\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 20 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 5 = 1000cm^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "feec31b3-1ad3-5875-8ef1-2ea93c1a154f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5377 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "974ddcf1-fc2c-505b-8c87-39b02be85d11" :step/math-academy-id 33545 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120807}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119890 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119710 :question/id #uuid "78d0bc32-ce61-5c58-a31a-bf3b0cc55761" :question/math-academy-id "e-8972"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d503d5d-ee8f-57a2-a6ef-b5b05d5e8ae4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051057}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233104}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Volume of a Rectangular Solid Given Its Dimensions"} :step/id #uuid "43fe0233-b488-5e80-a3a1-01c13e10309c" :step/math-academy-id 33546 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120808}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119891 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119711 :question/id #uuid "35043b46-40e9-51c4-8676-ad7a81763aae" :question/math-academy-id "e-8973"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a97dc9e3-b5a4-5913-94c3-aea43e639ce8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233105}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Dimension of a Rectangular Solid Given Its Volume and Some Other Dimensions"} :step/id #uuid "3554cb95-0998-5f0a-af81-3cb3a1724059" :step/math-academy-id 33547 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120809}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120809 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119892 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119712 :question/id #uuid "3528ee00-01a7-5c77-af81-50d53b5fa849" :question/math-academy-id "e-8974"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba5bf1d9-f6e2-5494-91fd-31c20d7fa09b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512898}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Rectangular Solid: Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "0abecfd9-a388-5a84-bdca-878bd2b73eb7" :step/math-academy-id 33548}] :activity/title "Volumes of Rectangular Solids" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119986}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173097} :activity/id #uuid "4e3e11c5-f10e-59a8-a6b4-34e2c5e85cc6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050142 :topic/id #uuid "30510c62-b4f5-5b4d-9d00-f64786d1c9a9" :topic/math-academy-id 3546} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173174 :tutorial/content "Recall that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the parabola's vertex.\n\nIf we are given the equation of a parabola in standard form, we can obtain its vertex form by completing the square.\n\nFor example, consider the parabola\n\n$$\ny= x^2+6x+16\n$$\n\nTo express the equation of this parabola in vertex form, we complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{2} + 6x + 16 \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 16 \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 3^{2} - 3^{2} + 16 \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 3^{2}) - 3^{2} + 16 \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2} - 3^{2} + 16 \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2} - 9 + 16 \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2} + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressed in vertex form, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (x - (-3))^{2} + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex of the parabola is at the point $({\\color{blue}-3},{\\color{red}7})$." :tutorial/id #uuid "98a78c68-d6fd-5642-a60b-1ac9e65d17ac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5186 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "19378221-e834-5c4b-990e-07c0375a2e82" :step/math-academy-id 458344 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173098}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172136 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171826 :question/id #uuid "6b2a8d64-5e70-598c-8baa-f7eb97ed0daf" :question/math-academy-id "e-7486"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b042103-9e24-53d9-bc20-80a9a90d191b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050372}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172479}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Equation of a Parabola in Vertex Form"} :step/id #uuid "51e398bc-e128-5132-8544-f247f071eb64" :step/math-academy-id 458345 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173099}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172137 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171743 :question/id #uuid "108ee1b6-2266-5956-b06d-4f4092fdee57" :question/math-academy-id "e-1469"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a23a10c1-a091-55bb-98a6-7718e46d841b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051078}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234061}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Equation of a Parabola With Leading Coefficients in Vertex Form"} :step/id #uuid "7c8b71e0-5a17-55a7-a089-43d023c422de" :step/math-academy-id 458349 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173100}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173100 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172138 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171825 :question/id #uuid "0a48df2c-c48e-5862-884f-3768abfbe5df" :question/math-academy-id "e-7478"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d374538-dedf-5a73-9cec-d30491eb1393" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172558}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Parabola Given Its Vertex and Leading Coefficient"} :step/id #uuid "cf650db5-7716-5a21-8630-34c4a846767d" :step/math-academy-id 458352 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173101}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173101 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173281 :tutorial/content "**Note**: You may find the algebra in this section a little tricky. It's a good idea to go through it, but you will not be quizzed on it. You may skip this section if you wish.\n\n![](images/2a/2a400b12675f8ebf19ba563192d9ae2296336e40ea380d40e6247564718a2b1f.png)\n\nYou may have seen already that for the parabola\n\n$$\ny=ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\na\\neq 0\n$$\n\nthe **axis of symmetry** of this parabola is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to derive this formula.\n\nSince\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe can take out a factor of $a$ from the right-hand side in the parabola formula:\n\n$$\ny = a\\left(x^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x + \\dfrac{c}{a} \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}x + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2}_{\\textrm{perfect square}} - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a} \\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a} \\right)^2 - \\dfrac{b^2-4ac}{4a^2} = \\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a} \\right)^2 + \\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a^2}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na((x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} + \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a^{2}}) &= a(x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} + a \\cdot \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a^{2}} \\\\\n&= a(x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} + \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a} \\\\\n&= a(x - (-\\frac{b}{2a})_(⏟)_(h))^{2} + \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a}_(⏟)_(k)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy denoting\n\n$$\nh=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}, \\qquad k=\\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a}\n$$\n\nwe get the equation of the parabola in vertex form:\n\n$$\ny = a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere the vertex is given by\n\n$$\n(h,k) = \\left(-\\dfrac{b}{2a}, \\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a} \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, since the parabola's axis of symmetry is the vertical line passing through the vertex, the equation of the axis must be\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "41e62e06-e589-55dc-846e-1177555ce582" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9116 :tutorial/title "Derivation of the Axis of Symmetry Formula"} :step/id #uuid "7ee3f8d6-f452-5eee-97bb-9be3735604ff" :step/math-academy-id 458355}] :activity/title "Writing the Equation of a Parabola in Vertex Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172214}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106917} :activity/id #uuid "75a4e4a8-271a-52f6-80d3-a6bbb0833cfd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050149 :topic/id #uuid "30edc058-70f2-5a72-9472-b7c90e677423" :topic/math-academy-id 1275} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107063 :tutorial/content "The formula for the area of a general triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\color{red}{a}}{\\color{blue}{b}}\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{red}a$ and $\\color{blue}b$ are two sides of the triangle and $C$ is the measure of the angle between those sides (called the **included angle**).\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nTo demonstrate how this works, suppose that we want to calculate the area of the non-right-angled triangle below:\n\n![](images/4b/4b0fb75f9b2eaf3d0237d8cfe2e31bc27b02425b6cffa8d15f27d6de03f60838.png)\n\nLet's call the lengths of the sides $BC = \\color{red}{a}$ and $AC ={\\color{blue}{b}}.$ For the triangle above we have\n${\\color{red}a=10},$ ${\\color{blue}b=12},$ and  $m\\angle C=45^{\\circ}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =60\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =30\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ff9f9791-f688-59c5-8c9d-b776016045be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2465 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e26490ba-2418-5a78-9c31-5425d1b67c42" :step/math-academy-id 463833 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106918}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105903 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105560 :question/id #uuid "62405619-31ca-5403-aa45-360e6b3fbd59" :question/math-academy-id "e-3289"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acc86fb6-2ef4-5f8f-b204-6e1fdbbf0530" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049449}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295123}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "ee7e485d-cba2-595c-b5bd-d8cd98cc7e04" :step/math-academy-id 463834 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105904 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105579 :question/id #uuid "c8d31ea4-41c6-5f24-bf7a-a950a30342d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4253"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cafdb077-0e02-5e7a-bf78-c3a8aacfb6f7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050847}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329721}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Angle or Side of a Triangle Given the Area of a Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "ca664e6a-cebd-5079-927f-7fc062b9c6fd" :step/math-academy-id 463837 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106920}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106920 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105905 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105561 :question/id #uuid "b41b2354-c339-5398-ab43-5886b8447c1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3294"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3173d361-b91d-5699-97d8-6595ef59d0a3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341859}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Quadrilateral"} :step/id #uuid "ab2a0c05-ebeb-54b0-a9a3-b37d1f143e15" :step/math-academy-id 463840 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106921}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106921 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105906 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105562 :question/id #uuid "472216c8-da7d-58fb-9056-e71d5e33460d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3297"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dd3dcff-06d2-5d61-9863-0f5b541d8d4b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341858}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle Given the Area of a Quadrilateral"} :step/id #uuid "85b3474e-aa3e-5a9b-8c47-069d6e314fad" :step/math-academy-id 463843 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106922}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106922 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107027 :tutorial/content "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b,$ and $c,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d30e12e2252b27a651bb02b718cf2214c7ddcf4f0e768c5bd952c11b2e4ecde.png)\n\nLet's now add the height of length $h$ perpendicular to $\\overline{BC},$ as shown.\n\n![](images/96/96817ab2168b9ec37777166a26001ee8bc7e7709f80fdf234ac6d70cdfbb5ddc.png)\n\nUsing the usual formula for the area $\\mathcal A$ of a triangle in terms of its base and height, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12 ah.\n$$\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{h}{b} = \\sin C\n$$\n\nwhich means that \n\n$$\nh = b\\sin C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting this into our expression for $\\mathcal A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12ab\\sin C\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar approach to prove the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}B\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e126d83f-0c01-514b-bb7e-ce2d16291b22" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13277 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Area of a General Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "29c5a503-b01d-5845-a29d-0bd76807b33f" :step/math-academy-id 463846}] :activity/title "The Area of a General Triangle" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105954}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176826} :activity/id #uuid "8f647d7f-d10f-52b0-a5c4-c95324d956b1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050269 :topic/id #uuid "3b1ff160-959d-503b-acf5-2be698a50baa" :topic/math-academy-id 3595} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176826 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177047 :tutorial/content "Given a dataset of paired numerical observations $(x,y)$ and the corresponding linear regression model\n\n$$\n\\widehat{y} =f(x)\n$$\n\nthe **residual** for a particular observation $(x, y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nResidual &= Actual - Estimated \\\\\n&= y - f(x) \\\\\n&= y - y^(^)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe subtract the *estimated* value predicted by the model from the actual *observed* value.\n\nTo illustrate this concept graphically, consider the following example.\n\nThe owner of an ice cream shop keeps track of the average daily temperature $x$ (measured in $^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$) and the resulting daily ice cream sales $y$ (in dollars). The scatter plot for several randomly selected days and the corresponding linear regression line are shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc149b8d911a7a9863df9cc25c635d6155accd676e83e335a65d9083596b779b.png)\n\nSuppose the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny= 2.5 x +165\n$$\n\nLet's use our regression line to compute the residual of the point with coordinates $\\color{red}(82, 360)$ (shown in red on our graph).\n\nFirst, notice that since the highlighted data point has coordinates ${\\color{red}(82, 360)}$, our actual value is\n\n$$\ny = {\\color{red}360}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the predicted value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^(^) &= 2.5x + 165 \\\\\n&= 2.5(82) + 165 \\\\\n&= 370\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/38/3843c71b50251ae035378507ffb22cfade48dff8e1544d8e1317e4e6d611f7a1.png)\n\nFinally, we compute the residual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nResidual &= Actual - Estimated \\\\\n&= y - y^(^) \\\\\n&= 360 - 370 \\\\\n&=-10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe residual tells us that the linear model *overestimates* the true volume of ice cream sales by $\\[math]10$ when the outside temperature is $82^\\circ\\,\\textrm{F}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "7e5cd2f9-494e-5533-98e9-c24f42631dad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5968 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "56d03218-9fd1-5cb5-a471-09641e54c0ea" :step/math-academy-id 36423 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176828}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176033 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175888 :question/id #uuid "bc4500bc-8992-5148-95eb-6f84ae2508cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-9717"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e77a14c-9943-5494-ae89-a77c7a1888d0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049868}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176342}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Residuals From a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "d5ff3834-5f43-5149-a054-8bfcbb8b3bbf" :step/math-academy-id 36424 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176829}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176828 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176034 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175889 :question/id #uuid "cb2190d0-5150-53ff-b7ab-ed364611bc8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9718"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0286d36b-217f-52e4-8580-e031b6dea31e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050210}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176340}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residuals Using the Regression Line Equation"} :step/id #uuid "2dbc11bb-69e2-5610-9c8e-28e53d57e103" :step/math-academy-id 36425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176827}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176829 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177048 :tutorial/content "A **residual plot** displays the values of the independent variable along the horizontal axis and the residuals along the vertical axis.\n\nThe scatter plot below represents data from a four-day sample on the number of people visiting a swimming pool and the average daytime temperature, in degrees Celsius. The line $\\ell$ is the corresponding linear regression line.\n\n![](images/62/6228578012fac7e42bedeb7ab7ad5fc9f42432aed8d5751b675df560bc867417.png)\n\nThese residuals for each of the four data points are highlighted below:\n\n![](images/d7/d70c44fe7b8fce43a8962ac104651acced75e10598e865a3b3c80d09f16ce234.png)\n\nIf we calculate the numerical values for the residuals at each data point (from left to right), we get the following results:\n\n$$\n-4, \\quad 8, \\quad -8, \\quad 4\n$$\n\nTo compute our residual plot, we plot each $x$-value and its corresponding residual on some coordinate axes:\n\n![](images/ff/ff8e6da5489313f1f981c379505f9a72610dfd2a4b5dc0113685d647ba02a1f3.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "ddf8fe09-fc0b-546d-942f-17a086708a11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5969 :tutorial/title "Residual Plots"} :step/id #uuid "e65742ab-c850-50fd-b1a4-0670ddc16c2b" :step/math-academy-id 36426 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176833}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176830 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176035 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175890 :question/id #uuid "3b40900e-ca06-5951-8261-aa13d8f966ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-9719"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa97e57a-70d8-5708-8b7d-fa05604a3f88" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233432}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Residual Plots"} :step/id #uuid "a7847754-3ac7-5ffa-af5a-838c5d3e8886" :step/math-academy-id 36427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176833 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177051 :tutorial/content "![](images/ff/ff8e6da5489313f1f981c379505f9a72610dfd2a4b5dc0113685d647ba02a1f3.png)\n\nWe should be aware of the following interpretations for residual plots:\n\n- Any point *above* the horizontal axis represents a *positive* residual. A positive residual means that the regression model *underestimates* the observed value for $y$ at this point.\n- Any point *below* the horizontal axis represents a *negative* residual. A negative residual means that the regression model *overestimates* the observed value for $y$ at this point.\n\nResidual plots can also be used to assess whether a trend line is a \"good\" fit for the corresponding data:\n\n- If a linear trend line is a good fit for the data, the residuals should be scattered randomly above and below the horizontal axis and be relatively close to zero.\n- In cases where the residuals show some clear pattern, this usually indicates that the data has no linear trend.\n\nThe residuals are scattered randomly about the horizontal axis in our last example. However, they are not close to zero, indicating that the trend line is a weak fit for the data." :tutorial/id #uuid "d7794124-93e8-5abb-8a78-4c8884a07547" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6034 :tutorial/title "Properties of Residual Plots"} :step/id #uuid "3fdb60d4-7e70-551a-9e4a-8bb61625c9cc" :step/math-academy-id 36805 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176830}}] :activity/title "Residuals and Residual Plots" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176138}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180350} :activity/id #uuid "83044b10-d4a9-5617-a56e-6d6330eeb3ae" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050310 :topic/id #uuid "3ef53278-7672-5742-99fe-d07cf78639fe" :topic/math-academy-id 3739} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180350 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180669 :tutorial/content "Remember that in order to add fractions with different denominators, like\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 4 + \\dfrac 1 8\n$$\n\nwe need to put the fractions over a **common denominator**, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{8} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2}{8} + \\frac{1}{8} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt works the same in algebra! To add two fractions, we need to put each fraction over a common denominator. Then, we add the numerators." :tutorial/id #uuid "f5b564ba-0934-5110-87ce-f04259821232" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3377 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2e650778-2844-52ef-8b57-d9a72869e882" :step/math-academy-id 18875 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180351}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180351 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179648 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179509 :question/id #uuid "0346b5d4-3d16-53e5-a25d-4f770ef5f863" :question/math-academy-id "e-5127"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63080bb8-7a6a-505d-813a-e6c99d337064" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050575}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348233}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding and Subtracting a Number and a Rational Expression"} :step/id #uuid "619ce5a9-f7df-5e65-b3c8-68102bc19437" :step/math-academy-id 18876 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180352}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180352 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179649 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179510 :question/id #uuid "866c1453-6d54-584c-ae65-0bc495ebf23a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5130"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3db20b82-9561-5c54-8e12-8914bb6747c3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338784}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding and Subtracting Rational Expressions With No Common Factors in the Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "833d312d-0148-58a3-bccf-4edf9a9c2020" :step/math-academy-id 18879}] :activity/title "Adding Rational Expressions With No Common Factors in the Denominator" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179887}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093324} :activity/id #uuid "708c0aaf-266b-5a9d-b352-10ed7b4b2883" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644 :topic/id #uuid "5beaefa1-1d02-51a5-90bc-250adca28a57" :topic/math-academy-id 986} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093324 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093430 :tutorial/content "The equation of the tangent line to the curve $y = f(x)$ at the point $P(x_1,y_1),$ in point-slope form, is given by\n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m (x - x_1), \\hspace{.75cm} m=f'(x_1).\n$$\n\nHere, $m = f'(x_1)$ because $f'(x_1)$ represents the slope of the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at the point $(x_1,y_1).$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the equation of the tangent line to the curve $y=x^2$ at the point $P(1,1).$\n\n![](images/48/487d21289b468fb82efa2097e425dd560811dc3165890509dacbab8093e0ecdb.png)\n\nSince $y=x^2,$ we have $f(x)=x^2.$ We begin by calculating the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t\t = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(x^2)= 2x\n$$\n\nThen, we determine the slope of the tangent at $P(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nm = f'(1)  = 2(1) = 2\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute the slope $m=2$ and the coordinates of $P(1,1)$ into the point-slope form, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =2(x-1) \\\\ y & =2x-2+1 \\\\ y & =2x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is $y=2x-1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8b0ed179-00e3-5e1a-84fc-c78773cfef19" :tutorial/math-academy-id 354 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4a6af753-c85c-59f9-be67-03ca672b3493" :step/math-academy-id 657 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093325}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093325 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092382 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091951 :question/id #uuid "2f5afc63-6988-5676-b170-5826e659a5fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-144"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db808f48-6d0c-5b35-9342-b1817df65172" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329858}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "dabdab57-40b6-5940-8089-29b4010d85ea" :step/math-academy-id 658 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093326}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093326 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092383 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091952 :question/id #uuid "e248bb90-ba83-5e3f-938b-2c8be7d6eb7a" :question/math-academy-id "e-146"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84c8ba29-95c9-5494-be0b-9b67bee6e45c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049394}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317252}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining where a Tangent Line Intersects an Axis"} :step/id #uuid "53c42827-7f73-5fef-997e-9f666fc35841" :step/math-academy-id 663}] :activity/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092460}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135006} :activity/id #uuid "e6580470-6b81-57e7-bf68-123e2ad0a485" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050659 :topic/id #uuid "5cfd2722-99a9-5eb4-adb4-6386be397f59" :topic/math-academy-id 2218} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134893 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135147 :tutorial/content "As we mentioned earlier, rigid motions are **distance-preserving**.\n\nFor example, if a rigid motion maps $\\triangle ABC$ to $\\triangle A'B'C'$, then the corresponding sides of the triangles must be congruent:\n\n$$\nAB = A'B', \\qquad AC = A'C', \\qquad BC = B'C'\n$$\n\nIn addition, rigid motions are **angle-preserving**, which means that the corresponding angles of the triangles must be congruent as well:\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = m\\angle A', \\qquad m\\angle B = m\\angle B', \\qquad m\\angle C = m\\angle C'\n$$\n\nSince all of the corresponding sides and angles are congruent, the triangles are congruent, and we write\n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC \\cong \\triangle A'B'C'\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4514dc0f-bb78-52d9-a728-8a09f7cbf667" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6157 :tutorial/title "Further Properties of Rigid Motions"} :step/id #uuid "6caa4091-01f6-57a9-8303-9c7ac2687088" :step/math-academy-id 37342 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134894 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135148 :tutorial/content "So, remember, there are *only three* types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n- Rotations\n- Reflections\n\nThere are two properties of rigid motions we need to know:\n\n- *A rigid motion maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.*\n- *For any pair of congruent polygons, there exists a rigid motion that maps one polygon onto another.*\n\nConsider the diagram below. Let's use the concept of rigid motions to explain why $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ are congruent.\n\n![](images/04/0487c9bb2a640a44e9ee67830937db4272c97f4a5f5101e43e3dcf3edd6c3a1c.png)\n\nNotice that $\\mathcal{Q}$ is the image of $\\mathcal{P}$ under the action of reflection in the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/18/183b182e26df36c6cfe6a9d682e0f3b000bff40ce7d405255a0abfee8529a946.png)\n\nSince $\\mathcal P$ is mapped to $\\mathcal Q$ under a rigid motion (reflection), $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ must be congruent." :tutorial/id #uuid "511ff35e-2666-5989-ad2f-e3e9fe541c7c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6163 :tutorial/title "Properties of Rigid Motions"} :step/id #uuid "1b767960-73f6-5f1a-86d5-06e3a151b4d8" :step/math-academy-id 37367 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135021 :tutorial/content "A **rigid motion** is any transformation that does not change the distances and angles between points. Rigid motions are also called **isometries**.\n\nThere are *only three* types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n- Rotations\n- Reflections\n\n**Watch out!** The following types of transformations are *not* rigid motions:\n\n- Dilations\n- Stretches" :tutorial/id #uuid "9208af14-0cfd-5ca9-989f-3bf18330cf12" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2294 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "66e47972-9ac9-5e15-abc6-6b2f033b161a" :step/math-academy-id 9555 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134894}} {:db