#edb/ref 17592186133549 :question/id #uuid "5fb05996-774b-5a89-9cc1-6876cc60338e" :question/math-academy-id "e-2912"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b01281fd-5272-579c-98d5-ca5cc00c73e4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050239}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234060}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Maps Presented in Function Notation That Are Rigid Motions"} :step/id #uuid "a91d4728-a024-5878-9599-76838230c6ed" :step/math-academy-id 9567 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134893}}] :activity/title "Rigid Motions and Congruence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134053}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193606} :activity/id #uuid "263de223-cabb-58ff-aad3-1a84df02f52a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050854 :topic/id #uuid "6c0cdb45-4687-5b06-beee-a13677429ccb" :topic/math-academy-id 4037} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193861 :tutorial/content "When drawing angles expressed in radians, it can be helpful to convert the angle to degrees first. This is especially helpful when writing trigonometric ratios in terms of reference angles, as we usually need to determine which quadrant the angle lies in.\n\nLet's express the following trigonometric ratio in terms of a reference angle:\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nYou might find it tricky to visualize this angle straight off the bat. So, let's start by converting the angle measure into degrees.\n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = 120^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $120^\\circ$, which lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\n![](images/34/342602449228f93042121b22f39d6cc203a6d945934a9ddb726e862838832815.png)\n\nNow, to express\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nin terms of $\\sin\\theta_R$, we follow three steps:\n\n1. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R$.\n2. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R$.\n3. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nis in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= π - θ \\\\\n&= π - \\frac{2π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3π}{3} - \\frac{2π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\n, and therefore we're interested in\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta_R = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nmust be *positive* because the sine ratio is *always* positive in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "4923e69b-5a86-59ae-ab93-5cd5e68e7ef0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6765 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f104bf84-a7fb-5c40-a540-f70c2282aa70" :step/math-academy-id 41036 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193607}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192468 :question/id #uuid "2f02b5d4-640f-5212-af28-66c572d47b37" :question/math-academy-id "e-10828"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "961a5c28-9e1e-5b0c-bd04-63068bebd8b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050795} {:db/id #edb/ref 175921